# `this.run = this.run.bind(this)` in a constructor: the read finds the method of the prototype alone, so bind makes a closure of a bound function over the object. Every later read of the field finds that closure alone, so a call of it is a direct call and handing it on does not hand on the method. A subclass that inherits the method changes nothing, though its objects come out of the same constructor through super(). A subclass that overrides the method, the new.target of another class, a getter under the name above the object, a constructor that stores on one path only and a deletion each end their part of it
# FORM: AsRead
# RUN: pipeline ChooseCallingConventions
# OPTIONS: check=VerifyEachPass only-passes=InsertObjectCoercibleChecks,ResolveAccesses,ResolveCalls,PlaceTypeTests,ChooseCallingConventions
# STATISTIC: BoundFunctions = 4

